                        "entry_id": "1ae71a66-7a80-3980-8108-a96e12845417",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A compost heap or dunghill."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8cd97555-cc41-4371-bd7b-aaf217738112",
                                "sense_id": "3237ca41-5957-3b84-9889-254d9ddf8a49",
                                "text": "[I]f we have fish at all / Let them be gold; and charge the gardeners now / To pick the faded creature from the pool, / And cast it on the mixen that it die."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f6771ae8-bb3b-4f1b-ac54-8b2602571e70",
                                "sense_id": "3237ca41-5957-3b84-9889-254d9ddf8a49",
                                "text": "Something of this occurred to him even in his wrath with me, for he spoke very softly to the filly, who now could scarce subdue herself; but she drew in her nostrils, and breathed to his breath and did all she could to answer him.\n'Not too hard, my dear,' he said: 'led him gently down on the mixen. That will be quite enough.' Then he turned the saddle off, and I was up in a moment."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "79fbf00f-6617-43ad-a94c-0cbe4eee6868",
                                "sense_id": "3237ca41-5957-3b84-9889-254d9ddf8a49",
                                "text": "\"I reckon that's the size o't,\" said Coggan, working along without looking up.\n\"Well, better wed over the mixen than over the moor,\" said Laban Tall, turning his sheep."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "03d2005b-493c-49da-8eb2-4d7c2abba715",
                                "sense_id": "3237ca41-5957-3b84-9889-254d9ddf8a49",
                                "text": "=To the chest they came, for the keys asked; manifest was their grudge, when therein they looked. Of those children he the heads cut off, and under the prison's mixen laid their bodies."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2ae93e34-0620-4a24-b5b2-9106ee69e4e1",
                                "sense_id": "3237ca41-5957-3b84-9889-254d9ddf8a49",
                                "text": "Close by the door stood the mixen, a collection of every abomination streams from which, in rainy weather, fertilized the lower meadows, generally the lord's pasture, and polluted the stream."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
